## To calculate PI, you can need a VCF file and a file including the sample IDs that are used in the VCF file.
## PI, aka the nucleotide diversity (π), is the average number of nucleotide differences per site between two sequences, within a population:
## the total pairwise differences at all sites / number of pairwise comparisons × number of sites

## To extract the IDs, you can use
bcftools query -l your_file.vcf.gz > samples.txt
## Then, you check the samples.txt and allocate them. It should look something like a tab-separated file, e.g.:
sample  population
ind1    spA
ind2    spA
ind3    spA
ind4    spB
ind5    spB
ind6    spB

## We will calculate PI using the whole-genome
## We use sinteractive with the default settings for this:
sinteractive
## For Population 1
vcftools \
  --gzvcf data.vcf.gz \
  --keep pop1.txt \
  --site-pi \
  --out pop1_pi_sites
## Oneliner
# vcftools --gzvcf Samples257_DP8.183inds.AN10.noScaff00-03_MS1.vcf.gz --keep aquiFile --site-pi --out aqui_pi_site
# 0.0521456

# Population 2
vcftools \
  --gzvcf data.vcf.gz \
  --keep pop2.txt \
  --site-pi \
  --out pop2_pi_sites
# vcftools --gzvcf Samples257_DP8.183inds.AN10.noScaff00-03_MS1.vcf.gz --keep adm1File --site-pi --out adm1_pi_site
# 0.0657245

## For both files, you get PI values all sites in the genome. Using awk below, we calculate the mean over all sites.
## Note, change the "$3" if necessary, dpeneding where the PI values are in the "XY.sites.pi"-file

awk 'NR>1 {sum += $3; n++} END {if (n>0) print sum/n}' pop1_pi_sites.sites.pi

## NR>1 skips the header.
## Output: single number = mean π across all listed sites (including those with PI = 0, aka invariant sites).
## With this, we will receive one genomw-wide PI value for all hybrid or F. aquilonia samples

-----
## Note: We can also calculate PI for each population in sliding windows
# Population 1
vcftools \
  --gzvcf data.vcf.gz \
  --keep pop1.txt \
  --window-pi 10000 \
  --window-pi-step 10000 \
  --out pop1_pi_10kb
